license: CC-BY-NC-4.0
abstract: >-
  This paper develops a formal framework for human function within the Unified
  Ontology of Human Function. It separates whole-person state, capacity,
  engagement-pattern specifications, actual engagement processes, potential,
  predicted and observed responses, evidence, problem hypotheses, governed
  actions and longitudinal update. It proposes a demand-bounded realizability
  criterion and an evidence-constrained architecture for auditable human-function
  reasoning under internal and external demands.
keywords:
  - human function
  - functional engagement
  - demand-bounded realizability
  - ontology
  - whole person
  - evidence-constrained decision support
  - internal demand
  - external demand
  - UOHF
